Installing OpenAlloy
====================

Copyright (C) 2026 Everton Vilhena Cardoso.  This file is free documentation;
see the notice at the end.


1. Requirements
---------------

  * Linux with systemd and udev (Debian, Ubuntu, SparkyLinux, Arch, CachyOS,
    Fedora and openSUSE are handled by the installer).
  * Python 3.10 or later.  The core (service and command line) uses only the
    Python standard library.
  * For the window: GTK 4.10 or later with PyGObject and pycairo
    (Debian: python3-gi, python3-gi-cairo, gir1.2-gtk-4.0).
  * pipx.
  * Optional, for the sound effects: parec (pulseaudio-utils) or pw-record.
    Both work with PipeWire.
  * A HyperX Alloy Origins Core TKL, USB ID 0951:16e6 (HyperX/Kingston) or
    03f0:098f (HP).  Without one, everything can be tried with --simulate.

Close OpenRGB, or disable the "HyperX Alloy Origins Core" device in it, before
using OpenAlloy: two programs writing to the same keyboard fight over it.


2. Quick install
----------------

As your normal user (not root), in the unpacked source directory:

    ./install.sh                  # English
    ./install.sh --lang pt_BR     # Brazilian Portuguese

Then unplug and replug the keyboard once and run:

    openalloy doctor

The installer asks for sudo only for the system packages, the udev rule and
the sleep hook.  It:

  1. installs the system packages (Python 3, pipx, PyGObject, GTK 4,
     gi-cairo, pulseaudio-utils);
  2. installs OpenAlloy with pipx, in an isolated environment that can see the
     distribution's PyGObject;
  3. installs /etc/udev/rules.d/60-openalloy.rules, which gives the logged-in
     user (uaccess) access to the two lighting interfaces only: interface 2
     (vendor channel) and interface 3 (HID LampArray).  The interfaces that
     carry what you type stay closed;
  4. installs, enables and restarts the systemd user service
     ~/.config/systemd/user/openalloy.service, so an upgrade takes effect at
     once;
  5. adds "OpenAlloy" to the application menu;
  6. installs the sleep hook /usr/lib/systemd/system-sleep/openalloy
     (/etc/systemd/system-sleep/openalloy when /usr is read-only);
  7. installs the manual pages openalloy(1) and openalloyd(1) in
     ~/.local/share/man/man1.

Installer options:

    --lang CODE     installer and interface language (en, pt_BR)
    --reactive      also install 61-openalloy-reactive.rules, which lets the
                    logged-in user read this keyboard's key events (needed by
                    the Reactive and Ripple effects and the reaction layer)
    --no-deps       do not install system packages
    --no-udev       do not install the udev rule and the sleep hook
    --no-service    do not install or enable the user service
    --no-desktop    do not add the menu entry
    --dev           editable install (pipx install -e)


3. Careful first install, step by step
--------------------------------------

This path is recommended the first time, and especially for the HP 03f0:098f.

  a) Try it without touching the hardware:

         python3 -m pytest -q                                 # if pytest is installed
         python3 -m openalloy.presentation.cli.main --simulate status
         python3 -m openalloy.presentation.cli.main --simulate-hp gui

  b) Install without starting the service:

         ./install.sh --no-service

     Replug the keyboard, then run 'openalloy doctor'.  It sends nothing to the
     keyboard; it lists the interfaces it found and their permissions.  On the
     HP 03f0:098f with firmware 21.16, interface 3 must show as
     "LampArray/Dynamic Lighting, rw".

  c) Test live RGB:

         openalloy test-rgb

     (or Keyboard > Test RGB in the window).  The keyboard must show the F-key
     row green, the left half red and the right half blue.

       - Yes: the protocol works with your firmware.  On keyboards with onboard
         memory, writing to it is unlocked for this unit and firmware version.
       - No, or nothing changed: writing stays locked.  Do not try loose HID
         commands; send the output of 'openalloy doctor' and
         'openalloy status' instead.

  d) Install the background service:

         ./install.sh

  e) Onboard memory (vendor channel only) is unlocked by step c.  To unlock it
     without the test (not recommended before the live test has worked), start
     the service with OPENALLOY_ENABLE_FLASH=I_ACCEPT_THE_RISK in its
     environment.


4. Upgrading
------------

Unpack the new version and run ./install.sh again; it restarts the service, so
the new version runs at once.  'openalloy status' shows the version in use.
Settings and presets in ~/.config/openalloy are kept.  To roll back, run the
install.sh of the previous version.

Upgrading from 1.5.3 or earlier: the udev rule of those versions never matched
(see NEWS, 1.5.4).  If OpenAlloy worked anyway, another rule is granting access
to the keyboard - possibly one from another tool that opens every interface,
including the ones that carry your keystrokes.  This read-only check lists
each hidraw node, its interface and who can open it:

    for n in /dev/hidraw*; do
      i=$(cat /sys/class/hidraw/$(basename $n)/device/../bInterfaceNumber 2>/dev/null)
      echo "$n if=$i $(udevadm info -q property $n | grep -E '^(ID_VENDOR_ID|CURRENT_TAGS)=' | tr '\n' ' ') $(getfacl -p $n 2>/dev/null | grep '^user:' | tr '\n' ' ') $(stat -c %a $n)"
    done
    grep -lE '0951|03f0|hidraw' /etc/udev/rules.d/* /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/* 2>/dev/null

If the keyboard's interfaces if=00 or if=01 are open to your user (an ACL
"user:...:rw-" or mode 666), remove the old rule once OpenAlloy 1.5.4 or later
is installed.


5. Without the installer
------------------------

    pipx install --system-site-packages .
    openalloy udev-rules | sudo tee /etc/udev/rules.d/60-openalloy.rules
    sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger
    install -Dm644 openalloy/resources/openalloy.service \
        ~/.config/systemd/user/openalloy.service
    systemctl --user daemon-reload && systemctl --user enable --now openalloy
    install -Dm644 -t ~/.local/share/man/man1 man/openalloy.1 man/openalloyd.1

uaccess applies to the active local session (not to SSH logins).


6. Uninstalling
---------------

    ./uninstall.sh            # keeps settings and presets
    ./uninstall.sh --purge    # also removes ~/.config/openalloy

Profiles already written to the keyboard's memory stay in the keyboard.


7. Known limits
---------------

  * On the HP 03f0:098f with firmware 21.16 only HID LampArray works: live
    colors, effects, painting, sound and key reaction, fixed color.  Onboard
    profiles, onboard writes and hardware brightness do not exist in that
    standard (brightness is done in software).
  * Key macros and hardware key remapping are not supported (the protocol is
    unknown); use keyd or input-remapper.  Game Mode keeps working on the
    keyboard itself (Fn + F12).
  * There is no firmware updater.
  * Do not use it together with OpenRGB, hxoctkl or another program that
    writes to the same HID interface.

